WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.640
In this video, you save cloud-init user data as a cloud configuration template and apply it to a new virtual machine, so every machine starts with the same settings.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:17.460
Under Advanced, Cloud Configuration Templates lists your saved templates. Select Create.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.480
Choose the namespace.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.760
Give the template a name.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:29.710
Keep the template type User Data. Network Data templates configure guest networking instead.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:39.270
Enter the cloud-init user data. It sets a password for the default user, allows password login, and installs the htop package.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.710
Select Create.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:47.980
Now create a virtual machine that uses the template. Go to Virtual Machines

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.550
and select Create.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.680
Choose the same namespace,

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.410
a name,

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.620
one CPU,

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.180
and two gigabytes of memory.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.560
On the Volumes tab,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.520
select the openSUSE Leap image.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.390
Open the Advanced Options tab.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:16.670
Under Cloud Configuration, open User Data Template and select the template you created.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:24.050
The editor now holds the template's user data. You can still adjust it for this virtual machine only.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:28.050
Select Create.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:37.030
The virtual machine is running. On first boot, cloud-init applies the password and installs the package.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:45.410
Every virtual machine that uses this template starts with the same configuration. Next, you create a virtual machine template.
